From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Moraza Sanchez <dmorazasanchezmendo@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 24 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] radv,nir,aco: BC-250 FSR4 V3 - isolation candidate 1 of 3

TESTING VARIANT. Not the patch shipped in patches/mesa.

Reverting the trimmed V3 patch back to upstream's full version restored
OptiScaler frame time from ~12 ms to ~8 ms, but four pieces were restored
at once, so which one mattered is unknown. This variant re-adds exactly
one of them on top of the trimmed base, so the responsible piece can be
identified by measurement instead of argument.

Candidate 1: the LDS spill-slot override in aco_spill.cpp (plus the
program->family plumbing it needs, which has no effect on its own).

Rationale for testing this first. In ACO,

    scratch_slots = slot_count - lds_slots;

so every spill slot that does not fit in LDS is spilled to scratch, and
scratch on this APU is shared system memory rather than dedicated VRAM.
The override raises the LDS budget from about 20 slots to 102 for shaders
already spilling hard, which moves roughly 80 slots of spill traffic off
system memory and onto on-die LDS. Losing occupancy is the deliberate
price. That is a far larger effect than anything the other candidates do.

Deliberately NOT included here:
  - imad24_ir3 / imul24 ACO cases and the MAD-chain lowering shapes
  - the explicit iadd(0, OpSDot) wrapper rules

If this variant measures ~8 ms, candidate 1 is the answer. If it measures
~12 ms, the next variant swaps in candidate 2 (imad24_ir3), then 3.

The deferred-round gate from the trimmed base is kept, since it only
changes how often radv_optimize_nir runs for shaders containing no packed
dot product at all and cannot affect FSR4 frame time either way.
